 Ruby's dotenv only allows `[A-Za-z0-9_.]` in key names, while Node's dotenv also permits `-`. godotenv has matched the Node charset since v1.4.0, so keys like `MY-VAR` parse cleanly here and in Node but will error under Ruby's dotenv.
 
+The charset is ASCII in all three. A name holding anything else is an error
+here, and the error names the character it found, so a file with a key of
+`Ω` reports `unexpected character "Ω" in variable name`; Ruby's and
+Node's dotenv ignore such a line instead.
+
+### Byte order marks
+
+A UTF-8 byte order mark at the start of a file is skipped, so an env file saved
+by a Windows editor loads exactly like one without it. Ruby's dotenv does the
+same by reading env files as `rb:bom|utf-8`, and Node's dotenv treats `U+FEFF`
+as leading whitespace in its line pattern. Only a mark at the very start is a
+byte order mark: a `U+FEFF` anywhere else is a stray character in a variable
+name and is an error.
+
+### Variable substitution
+
+A value can reference a variable defined earlier in the same file, or one
+already in the environment. `$NAME` and `${NAME}` both work, names resolve
+case-insensitively, and an undefined name expands to the empty string.
+
+```shell
+BASE=https://example.com
+ENDPOINT=$BASE/v1
+QUOTED="${BASE}/v2"
+LITERAL='$BASE stays as written'
+ESCAPED="\$BASE stays as written"
+NOT_A_COMMAND="$(BASE) stays as written"
+```
+
+Substitution happens in unquoted and double quoted values. Single quoted values
+are literal, a backslash escapes a single `$`, and `$(` is left alone because it
+opens command substitution rather than a variable reference.
+
+An unbraced `$NAME` ends at the first character outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, so
+`$BASE/v1` and `$BASE-suffix` keep their trailing text. Braces take the full key
+charset, so `${MY.KEY}` and `${MY-KEY}` resolve where the unbraced forms cannot.
+
 ### Precedence & Conventions
 
 Existing envs take precedence of envs that are loaded later.
