 If you need to, you can also use `godotenv.Overload()` to defy this convention
 and overwrite existing envs instead of only supplanting them. Use with caution.
 
+When you pass several files to a single call, the three entry points resolve a
+key defined in more than one of them differently, and they do not agree:
+
+| Call | Winner for a shared key | Why |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `Load("a.env", "b.env")` | `a.env` | nothing already set is replaced, and `a.env` sets it first |
+| `Overload("a.env", "b.env")` | `b.env` | everything is replaced, so the last write stands |
+| `Read("a.env", "b.env")` | `b.env` | each file is merged over the ones before it |
+
+So `Read` does not preview what `Load` will do once more than one file is
+involved. Within a single file all three keep the last definition of a key.
+
+Loading several files is not atomic. A failure on the third file leaves the
+first two in the environment, and the call reports the error without undoing
+them; `Read` likewise returns the keys it had merged before the failure rather
+than an empty map. The file that fails contributes nothing when it cannot be
+opened or parsed, since each file is parsed in full before any of it is
+applied, and can contribute part of itself only when the operating system
+refuses one of its variables, in which case the keys it applied before the
+refusal vary between runs.
+
